;;; `std.contradiction/clash-0` — a fact of no argument beside its negation:
;;; the rule concludes `(false)`.
;;;
;;; ── What this file and its six siblings can and cannot say ─────────────────
;;;
;;; A state that holds a fact and its stored negation is dead, and the kernel
;;; says so of every fact with no rule. `clash-0` to `clash-6` state the same
;;; contradiction as a rule, one a count of arguments, and what the rule adds
;;; is not the verdict: it is the fact `(false)`, derived, with the pair as
;;; its premises.
;;;
;;; So the claim below, `(false)`, *every node is dead*, holds of this program
;;; with the rule and without it, and no claim made of facts can tell the two
;;; apart: a dead state has no model to list. What the file gives the rule is
;;; a program in which it **fires**, which is what the coverage gate asks
;;; (`ein-infer/tests/stdlib_coverage.rs`), and the firing itself, its
;;; premises the pair and its conclusion `(false)`, a fact root then holds,
;;; is asserted in-process, in `ein-infer/tests/contradiction_rules.rs`.
;;;
;;; The file where a claim *can* see these rules is
;;; `08_clash_satisfied.ein`: all seven loaded, activated, and silent, over
;;; facts and denials that do not meet. A rule that fired there would turn a
;;; model into a contradiction.
;;;
;;; ── One rule a file ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Seven rules reach one verdict, so each firing file activates one of them
;;; and no other: here `clash-0`, for the one relation.

(import std.contradiction :symbols (clash-0))

(relation raining :why "it is raining")

(clash-0 raining)

(raining)
(not (raining))          ; ⊥ — the fact and its denial

;; Root alone (Q-M1f.254): the question is root's verdict.
(solve
  :no-search
  :expect (false))

(query
  :goal (raining))
